/* test_ml_linreg_exact.js -- the LinearRegression solver accuracy contract.
 *
 * WHAT THE SOLVER PROMISES (the docs state the same):
 *   1. For a design the solver ACCEPTS, the coefficients ARE the ordinary
 *      least-squares solution up to rounding of the normal equations. The
 *      forced-optimum battery below pins that at <= 1e-9 relative (measured
 *      3e-16 .. 6e-10 across feature scales 1 .. 1e3); the independent oracle
 *      is numpy.linalg.lstsq (SVD), whose values are embedded (generated by
 *      tests/agent/t2d_ml_df_time/gen_linreg_oracle.py).
 *   2. The un-ridged system is accepted only while every pivot clears
 *      DYN_SOLVE_MIN_PIVOT = 1000*eps (~2.2e-13) of the largest diagonal
 *      entry of A^T A -- a cut that separates the elimination's own
 *      ROUNDING-LEVEL residue (measured <= ~2.4e-17 relative across the
 *      singular battery below; that residue IS rank deficiency and must go to
 *      the ridge) from a WELL-POSED design's genuine small singular value,
 *      which the normal equations solve to rounding. Below the cut the ridge
 *      solves: A^T A + lI with l = DYN_RIDGE = 1e-9, ABSOLUTE in the
 *      WEIGHT-NORMALIZED system (the system is divided by the mean sample
 *      weight first, so the load is 1e-9 x mean weight in raw units, and no
 *      product can overflow at 1e300 weights or underflow to 0 at subnormal
 *      ones). The load carries NO factor of the system's own scale: a
 *      scale-proportional load would zero every direction sitting far below
 *      the largest column -- the mixed-unit rows of section 7 pin that. The
 *      coefficients are then ridge-biased -- only IDENTIFIABLE quantities
 *      (predictions on consistent targets) are pinned, at <= 1e-4 on the band
 *      battery below.
 *   3. A SINGULAR design (duplicate/constant/collinear columns, identical
 *      rows, a zero column) does not throw, however its elimination rounds:
 *      its pivots sit at the rounding level the gate refuses, so the ridge
 *      fallback reaches them -- which an elimination refusing only exact
 *      zeros used to "solve" into 1/residue garbage. Section 5 is that band.
 *   4. The two input forms agree BIT-FOR-BIT: a dense fit and a CSR fit of
 *      the same reading produce identical coefficients (Object.is), on the
 *      un-ridged path and on the fallback alike.
 *   5. The weighted identities hold on the accepted path: all-ones weights
 *      are bit-identical to no weights, and a power-of-two weight scale is
 *      bit-identical to unit weights (the load is homogeneous in w).
 *   6. Well-posed near-singular designs ABOVE the gate solve EXACTLY
 *      (<= 1e-12, section 7) -- including when the target's true coefficients
 *      lie along the near-null direction, exactly what a ridge bias would
 *      shrink. Designs BELOW the gate (genuine rank deficiency, and the
 *      exactly-singular mixed-unit scales) are ridge-biased and pinned at
 *      <= 1e-6 on their identifiable quantities.
 *
 * Every system here is EXACTLY solvable by construction (integer design,
 * integer coefficients, integer targets), so `want`/`oraclePred` is the true
 * OLS answer and any deviation is solver rounding -- which is exactly what is
 * pinned. The band rows additionally carry the minimum-norm coefficients the
 * solver must not amplify past.
 *
 * Run: dynajs tests/test_ml_linreg_exact.js
 */
